:Whether a wishing fountain is ever installed or not, I like this idea
:of a way of granting wishes a lot, and I think it should be applied to
:_all_ wishing.  If your wish is granted, then the item you wish for
:appears...somewhere in the next five levels you visit.  If you are
:already in Hell, then at least you have to poke around the Hell level
:to find it.  This sure would help restore the game balance a wand of
:wishing messes up, and restore a sense of fun to the wishing process.
:
:A similar idea should apply to gifts granted by your god.  This would
:help quash the habit of hanging around an altar doing sacrifice after
:sacrifice in the old and revered Monty Haul campaign technique;
:especially if there were ambiguous hints as to whether the god(dess)
:had actually granted a material boon.
:
:Comments?
     I love it!  Adds risk, too: if you wish for a Stormbringer, carry
some missile weapons and be either invisible or able to jump: you're
likely to find it in the hands of something on the order of a fire giant
before you reach it.
     I don't think that should be the case for God-granted wishes,
though.  Perhaps spellbooks and such, but *all* the offering-related gifts
are named weapons.  It's supposed to be a *good* thing to get something
from your God, not a death trap (I've gotten Stormbringer as a 7th level
character on dlvl 5.  Can you imagine trying to deal with a mordor orc
wielding, say, Mjollnir?)
     Perhaps it could appear under a boulder somewhere, if there's one
on the level; otherwise, it'd show up at the altar.  Only giants can
steal things from under boulders, and that's not too likely.  (I usually
stow things under boulders, but an episode with a hill giant and
Stormbringer taught me to keep the named weapons in chests except on
very shallow dlvls.)
     Still trying to make it to that (now cursed) magic lamp on the
dlvl 4 bones with a priest.... (I can get past most monsters by kicking
them, but those damn soldier ants keep screwing up my game on dlvl 2 or
3).  What to wish for, I wonder, now.

